Day 22: PostgreSQL – DDL & DML Basics

Topic:
Today’s session focused on foundational PostgreSQL operations, including Data Definition Language (DDL) commands for creating and managing database structures and Data Manipulation Language (DML) commands for handling data. We explored data types specific to PostgreSQL, such as SERIAL, TEXT, and BOOLEAN, and recreated VolyoDB tables in PostgreSQL to understand syntax differences.

Learning Objectives:

• Understand PostgreSQL DDL commands to create and manage tables.
• Learn PostgreSQL-specific data types and their usage.
• Practice basic DML operations for inserting and managing data.

Activities / Deliverables:

• Recreate VolyoDB tables in PostgreSQL.
• Insert sample data into the recreated tables.
• Compare PostgreSQL syntax with other database systems.

Important Concepts:

• DDL commands define database structures (e.g., CREATE TABLE).
• DML commands manipulate data (e.g., INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE).
• PostgreSQL-specific data types like SERIAL (auto-increment) and BOOLEAN.

SQL Syntax / Commands:

• CREATE TABLE table_name (column_name SERIAL PRIMARY KEY, column_name TEXT, column_name BOOLEAN);
• INSERT INTO table_name (column1, column2) VALUES (value1, value2);

Real-world Use Cases:

• Building and managing relational databases for applications.
• Migrating databases from other systems to PostgreSQL.

Tools / Tech:

• PostgreSQL
• pgAdmin 4

Summary:
Today’s session provided hands-on experience with PostgreSQL DDL and DML commands, emphasizing data types and syntax differences. We recreated VolyoDB tables and performed basic data operations, reinforcing foundational database management skills.

